   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.4. Increased capacity of Member States to support digital transitions of skills development systems and develop digital skills</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">From February 2023 &#x2013; June 2023 Learning Lions, an ILO Implementing partner for Digital Skills and Jobs reviewed and developed for Technical and Vocational Education and Training Authority (TVETA), the digital skills curriculum in line with Market demand, following a Digital Assessment which was done in consultation with constituents and other stakeholders. TVETA is currently reviewing the draft curriculum for standardization and accreditation. By means of resources from the Netherlands, the Ministry of Foreign Affairs funded Inclusive jobs and education for refugees and host communities (PROSPECTS) Kenya (KEN/19/51/NLD), the ILO provided technical support from May to November 2022 for the assessment of digital skills supply and digital jobs demand for refugees and host communities in Kenya for the purposes of informing the review of digital skills curriculum in line with market demand. Further, this assessment informed the partnership with an implementing partner (i.e., Learning Lions) leading to the review of their Digital Skills Curriculum addressing training at three levels; i) Mobile Leap, designed to introduce youths to technology while emphasizing personal and professional development skills needed for the digital age., ii) Boot Camp,  that allows specialisations in web development, graphic design and animation, while providing the basic training in navigating the web and working professionally on a computer. and iii) Advanced skills, which is an assisted autodidact learning approach on a set of curriculum modules that leads trainees to acquire concrete, sellable skills in their areas of specialization. The Technical and Vocational Education and Training Authority (TVETA) is reviewing the curriculum for accreditation and recognition of Prior Learning. Once approved, the training programmes will target 210 youth and 50 candidates for RPL in the pilot phase and later roll out to other institutions.</narrative>
   </description>
