  <result aggregation-status="0" type="2">
   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Output 5.2. Increased capacity of Member States to strengthen skills and lifelong learning policies, governance models and financing system</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">From January 2023 &#x2013; May 2023, the Ministry of Education and Technical and Vocational Education and Training Authority (TVETA) reviewed and validated the Competency-Based Education, Training and Assessments (CBETA) Standards and Centre of Excellence (CoE) standards. The two standards provide guidelines for the development of CBET Curriculum and the establishment of CoE. This is in line with gender-responsive National Skills Development Policy 2020, developed by the Government of Kenya through the Ministry of Education, Ministry of Labour and Social Protection and the then State Department of Post Training and Skills Development, using ILO standards related to skills development and sectoral approach to skills development. With Support from the United States Department of Labour (USDOL) funded the Better Utilization of Skills for Youth through Quality Apprenticeship (BUSY), in February 2020, the ILO provided technical support for the development of a draft National Skills Development Policy which was validated by stakeholders and currently under approval. From the Policy, the Ministry developed the National Skills development bill in 2021 also validated and under approval.  
With support from the Netherlands, the Ministry of Foreign Affairs funded Inclusive jobs and education for refugees and host communities (PROSPECTS) Kenya (KEN/19/51/NLD), From January to May 2023, the ILO provided technical support  to  TVETA to develop CBETA and CoE standards which were validated and approved by TVETA board and currently undergoing quality approval by Kenya Bureau of Standards. The ILO is currently providing support to TVETA to develop implementation tools to operationalize these standards.</narrative>
   </description>
